# Template for .env. NEVER commit .env (it is gitignored).
#
# The supported way to create this file is the credential wizard, which
# prompts for the password silently so it never reaches your terminal
# scrollback, your shell history, or a Claude transcript (RULE-008):
#
#   python3 -m vcfops_common setup
#
# Creating it by hand also works: copy this file to .env, fill in real
# values, and chmod 600 it.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Every assignment below is commented out ON PURPOSE. Uncomment only the
# profile you actually use and replace the placeholder values. If the
# placeholders were left live, a straight copy of this file would look
# like three fully-configured profiles to the preflight doctor while
# every one of them pointed at a host that does not exist.
#
# Do NOT source this file into your shell. The CLIs load .env themselves
# (src/vcfops_common/_env.py), so `--profile <name>` is all they need.
# Sourcing exports every secret into that shell, where any later `env`,
# `printenv`, or error dump prints them (RULE-008).
#
# Quoting: an unquoted value is truncated at the first `#` (it is read as an
# inline comment). If a password contains `#`, or leading/trailing spaces,
# wrap it in single quotes: VCFOPS_PROD_PASSWORD='p#ss word'. The wizard
# handles this for you.
#
# Credentials are profile-prefixed: VCFOPS_<PROFILE>_*. There is no
# fallback to flat VCFOPS_HOST / VCFOPS_USER / VCFOPS_PASSWORD; that was a
# hard cutover (_env.py). Profile names are yours to choose; the three
# below are the convention this repo's docs assume. For which profile each
# command defaults to, see knowledge/context/dictionary.md (the two-lab
# policy) and knowledge/rules/no-destructive-on-prod.md (RULE-009).

# --- VCF Operations: prod profile (primary lab, read-only recon) ---
#VCFOPS_PROD_HOST=vcfops.lab.example.com
#VCFOPS_PROD_USER=svc-claude-poc
#VCFOPS_PROD_PASSWORD=change-me
#VCFOPS_PROD_AUTH_SOURCE=Local
#VCFOPS_PROD_VERIFY_SSL=false

# --- VCF Operations: qa profile (primary lab, admin, uninstall round-trips) ---
#VCFOPS_QA_HOST=vcfops.lab.example.com
#VCFOPS_QA_USER=admin
#VCFOPS_QA_PASSWORD=change-me
#VCFOPS_QA_AUTH_SOURCE=Local
#VCFOPS_QA_VERIFY_SSL=false

# --- VCF Operations: devel profile (devel lab, admin, destructive playground) ---
#VCFOPS_DEVEL_HOST=vcfops-devel.lab.example.com
#VCFOPS_DEVEL_USER=admin
#VCFOPS_DEVEL_PASSWORD=change-me
#VCFOPS_DEVEL_AUTH_SOURCE=Local
#VCFOPS_DEVEL_VERIFY_SSL=false

# Default active profile when a command has no per-command default and
# --profile is not passed. Setting this affects ALL invocations, including
# destructive ones, so prefer passing --profile explicitly.
#VCFOPS_PROFILE=prod

# --- Synology DSM (Tier 2 management pack development) ---
# Only needed if you are developing or testing the Synology adapter.
#SYNO_HOST=nas.lab.example.com
#SYNO_PORT=5001
#SYNO_USER=svc-monitoring
#SYNO_PASSWORD=change-me
#SYNO_VERIFY_SSL=false

# --- Tier 2 SDK build (optional) ---
# VCFCF_SDK_JAR points at the Broadcom adapter SDK jar when building a Tier 2
# pak locally. The official pak build is the adapter repo's own CI on a v* tag
# (RULE-014), which fetches this itself; you only need it for local previews.
# VCFCF_SDK_JAR=/path/to/vrops-adapters-sdk.jar
#
# VCFCF_RELEASE_BUILD=1 is set by that CI to stamp the real adapter.yaml
# version. Leave it unset locally: a dev build then stamps 0.0.0.<build_number>
# so a hand-built pak can never be version-indistinguishable from a release.
